  "output": "Now I have a complete picture of the codebase. Here is the performance analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis: HR Policy Bot\n\nThis is a Python/FastAPI backend — there are no React components, so the re-render category is N/A. All other categories have real findings.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Connection-Per-Call Anti-Pattern (Highest Impact)\n\n**`src/data/db.py`** — Every public method opens and closes its own `aiosqlite` connection. A single query flow triggers at minimum 5 separate connection open/close cycles:\n\n```\nprocess_query() calls:\n  → db.save_query()              # open/close\n  → (8 specialists run)\n  → db.save_query() again        # open/close  (update via INSERT OR REPLACE)\n  → db.save_orchestrator_response()  # open/close\n```\n\nAnd inside `FetchCache`, each `get(url)` / `set_cached()` also opens its own connection through `DatabaseManager`.\n\n**Fix:** Hold a persistent connection and use WAL mode. Simplest approach that preserves the current structure:\n\n```python\n# src/data/db.py\n\nclass DatabaseManager:\n    def __init__(self, db_path: Path = _DB_PATH) -> None:\n        self._db_path = db_path\n        self._db_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        self._db: aiosqlite.Connection | None = None\n        self._lock = asyncio.Lock()\n\n    async def initialize(self) -> None:\n        self._db = await aiosqlite.connect(self._db_path)\n        self._db.row_factory = aiosqlite.Row\n        await self._db.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL\")\n        await self._db.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL\")\n        await self._db.executescript(_SCHEMA)\n        await self._db.commit()\n        logger.info(\"Database initialized at %s\", self._db_path)\n\n    async def close(self) -> None:\n        if self._db:\n            await self._db.close()\n            self._db = None\n\n    # Replace every `async with aiosqlite.connect(...) as db:` block with `db = self._db`\n    async def save_query(self, record: QueryRecord) -> None:\n        await self._db.execute(\"INSERT OR REPLACE INTO queries ...\", (...))\n        await self._db.commit()\n```\n\nAlso add to the schema (run once at `initialize()`):\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_agent_responses_query_id ON agent_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_orchestrator_responses_query_id ON orchestrator_responses(query_id);\nCREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_feedback_query_id ON feedback(query_id);\n```\n\nWithout these indexes, every `get_agent_responses(query_id)` does a full table scan.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Policy Registry Read N×1 Pattern\n\n**`src/agents/specialist.py:72-75`** — `_load_instruments()` opens and JSON-parses `policy_registry.json` once per specialist, eight times total at startup:\n\n```python\ndef _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:          # disk read\n        registry = json.load(f)              # full JSON parse\n    return [r for r in registry if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n**Fix:** Cache at module level:\n\n```python\n# src/agents/specialist.py\n\n_registry_cache: list[dict] | None = None\n\ndef _get_registry() -> list[dict]:\n    global _registry_cache\n    if _registry_cache is None:\n        with open(_REGISTRY_PATH) as f:\n            _registry_cache = json.load(f)\n    return _registry_cache\n\nclass SpecialistAgent(GenericAgent):\n    def _load_instruments(self, agent_id: str) -> list[dict]:\n        return [r for r in _get_registry() if agent_id in r[\"agent_ids\"]]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Gemini Model Object Rebuilt on Every LLM Call\n\n**`src/llm/client.py:40-50`** — `_make_model()` is called by `chat()` and `chat_parsed()` on every request, constructing a new `GenerativeModel` each time. With a semaphore of 3, up to 3 model objects are built concurrently for each batch of specialist calls (8 specialists × 2 LLM phases = 16 model constructions per user query).\n\n```python\nasync def chat(self, system_prompt: str, user_message: str, model: str | None = None) -> str:\n    m = self._make_model(model)   # ← new object every call\n    ...\n```\n\n**Fix:** Cache by `(model_name, json_output)`:\n\n```python\nclass GeminiClient:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        ...\n        self._model_cache: dict[tuple, genai.GenerativeModel] = {}\n\n    def _make_model(self, model: str | None = None, json_output: bool = False) -> genai.GenerativeModel:\n        key = (model or self._model_name, json_output)\n        if key not in self._model_cache:\n            generation_config: dict = {\n                \"temperature\": self._config.model.temperature,\n                \"top_p\": self._config.model.top_p,\n            }\n            if json_output:\n                generation_config[\"response_mime_type\"] = \"application/json\"\n            self._model_cache[key] = genai.GenerativeModel(\n                model_name=key[0],\n                generation_config=generation_config,\n            )\n        return self._model_cache[key]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Global Lock Serializes All Domain Rate-Limit Checks\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:79-84`** — `_domain_lock` is a single lock shared across all domains. When 8 specialists concurrently call `fetch()`, they all serialize at this lock even for completely different domains:\n\n```python\nasync def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n    domain = self._get_domain(url)\n    async with self._domain_lock:     # ← ALL domains wait here\n        last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n        delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n        if delay_required > 0:\n            await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)   # sleeping inside a global lock!\n        self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n```\n\nSleeping inside a global lock is particularly bad — it blocks all other domains for the full delay duration.\n\n**Fix:** Use per-domain locks:\n\n```python\nclass FetchEngine:\n    def __init__(self, ...) -> None:\n        ...\n        self._domain_locks: dict[str, asyncio.Lock] = defaultdict(asyncio.Lock)\n        # remove self._domain_lock\n\n    async def _fetch_with_concurrency(self, url: str) -> str:\n        domain = self._get_domain(url)\n        async with self._domain_locks[domain]:   # per-domain, not global\n            last_request = self._domain_last_request[domain]\n            delay_required = 1.0 - (time.time() - last_request)\n            if delay_required > 0:\n                await asyncio.sleep(delay_required)\n            self._domain_last_request[domain] = time.time()\n        async with self._domain_semaphores[domain]:\n            return await self._do_fetch(url)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. In-Memory Cache Layer Missing\n\nEvery cache lookup (`FetchCache.get()`) goes to SQLite on disk. Policy documents are large and frequently re-fetched within a session since the TTL is days/weeks but every new query re-hits the DB.\n\n**Fix:** Add an LRU in-memory layer in `FetchCache`:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass FetchCache:\n    _MEM_CACHE_SIZE = 64  # hold 64 URLs in memory\n\n    def __init__(self, ...) -> None:\n        ...\n        self._mem: OrderedDict[str, CacheEntry] = OrderedDict()\n\n    async def get(self, url: str) -> CacheEntry | None:\n        if url in self._mem:\n            self._mem.move_to_end(url)\n            return self._mem[url]\n        cached = await self._db_manager.get_cached(url)\n        if cached is None:\n            return None\n        entry = CacheEntry(\n            url=cached.url, content=cached.content_text,\n            ttl_hours=cached.ttl_hours, status_code=cached.status_code,\n            content_length=cached.content_length,\n        )\n        self._mem[url] = entry\n        if len(self._mem) > self._MEM_CACHE_SIZE:\n            self._mem.popitem(last=False)\n        return entry\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. `content_hash` Recomputes SHA-256 on Every Access\n\n**`src/fetch/cache.py:38-39`** — `content_hash` is a `@property` that runs SHA-256 on the full document content every time it's accessed. It's only called once in `set_cached()`, but the pattern is fragile.\n\n```python\n@property\ndef content_hash(self) -> str:\n    return hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()   # O(N) every access\n```\n\n**Fix:** Compute once and cache:\n\n```python\n@dataclass\nclass CacheEntry:\n    url: str\n    content: str\n    ttl_hours: int\n    status_code: int | None = None\n    content_length: int | None = None\n    _content_hash: str | None = field(default=None, init=False, repr=False)\n\n    @property\n    def content_hash(self) -> str:\n        if self._content_hash is None:\n            self._content_hash = hashlib.sha256(self.content.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()\n        return self._content_hash\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. `re` Module Imported Inside Hot-Path Method\n\n**`src/fetch/engine.py:69`** — `import re` is inside `_normalize_url()`, which is called on every `fetch()`:\n\n```python\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    import re    # ← imported on every call\n    if re.search(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$', url):\n```\n\nPython's import system caches after the first load so this won't re-execute the module code, but it still performs a dict lookup in `sys.modules` + attribute resolution on every call. Move `import re` to the top of the file and compile the pattern once:\n\n```python\n# top of engine.py\nimport re\n_LAWS_LOIS_RE = re.compile(r'laws-lois\\.justice\\.gc\\.ca/eng/acts/[^/]+/$')\n\n# in class\ndef _normalize_url(self, url: str) -> str:\n    if _LAWS_LOIS_RE.search(url):\n        return url.rstrip('/') + '/FullText.html'\n    return url\n```\n\n---\n\n### 8. Double `save_query` Write Per Request\n\n**`src/api/websocket.py:162, 182`** — `save_query` is called twice: once before processing (with empty `agents_invoked`) and once after to update it. Both do a full `INSERT OR REPLACE`, re-writing the entire row. With the connection-per-call pattern, this is two connection open/close cycles.\n\nOnce the persistent connection fix (#1) is applied, add an explicit `UPDATE` for the second write instead of re-inserting:\n\n```python\n# Add to DatabaseManager\nasync def update_query_completion(\n    self,\n    query_id: str,\n    agents_invoked: list[str],\n    overall_confidence: str | None,\n    processing_time_ms: int,\n) -> None:\n    await self._db.execute(\n        \"\"\"UPDATE queries SET agents_invoked=?, overall_confidence=?, processing_time_ms=?\n           WHERE query_id=?\"\"\",\n        (json.dumps(agents_invoked), overall_confidence, processing_time_ms, query_id),\n    )\n    await self._db.commit()\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Issue | File | Impact | Effort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Connection-per-call + no WAL | `db.py` | High — 5+ connections per request | Medium |\n| Missing indexes on `query_id` FK columns | `db.py` schema | High — full table scans | Low |\n| Policy registry parsed 8× at startup | `specialist.py:72` | Low (startup only) | Trivial |\n| Gemini model object rebuilt per call | `client.py:40` | Medium — 16+ allocations per query | Trivial |\n| Global lock serializes domain rate checks | `engine.py:79` | High — serializes all concurrent fetches | Low |\n| No in-memory cache layer | `cache.py` | Medium — every cache hit hits SQLite | Medium |\n| SHA-256 in `@property` with no memoization | `cache.py:38` | Low | Trivial |\n| `import re` inside method + uncompiled pattern | `engine.py:69` | Low | Trivial |\n| Double INSERT on query record | `websocket.py:162,182` | Low | Low |\n\nThe three highest-impact changes are: **persistent DB connection with WAL mode**, **per-domain locks instead of global lock**, and **adding FK indexes** — those will have the most measurable effect under concurrent load.\n",
